  "summary": "Daring Club Motema Pembe (DCMP), based in Kinshasa, was founded in 1936 by missionary and educator Father Raphaël de la Kethulle de Ryhove. First called Daring, the club became CS Imana in 1949 and adopted the name Daring Club Motéma Pembe in 1985. Its early players came largely from Sainte-Anne school, and the club developed through a network of youth and feeder teams.\n\nAfter early setbacks and internal disputes, CS Imana became a major force in the 1950s, winning national championships in 1955 and 1956. Star player Paul Bonga Bonga later joined Belgium’s Standard de Liège. The club went on to collect numerous domestic titles, including 12 Linafoot championships and a record 14 Congo Cups, according to the honours listed.\n\nDCMP also made history as the first Congolese club to compete in the African Cup of Champions Clubs, in 1965. Its greatest continental achievement came in 1994, when it won the African Cup Winners’ Cup; it later lost the CAF Super Cup to Espérance de Tunis. In the 2010s, organizational turmoil led to a split and the formation of a separate CS Imana, while leadership changes sought to restore DCMP’s competitiveness.",
